## Original Content
<p>As federal borrowing rules change, graduate education has a chance to move beyond sticker price and focus on flexibility, transparency, and long term value. For students, the question is not only how to pay for a degree, but whether the degree is designed to be worth the investment.</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://blog.coursera.org/as-u-s-graduate-financing-changes-affordability-by-design-matters-more/">As U.S. graduate financing changes, affordability by design matters more</a> appeared first on <a href="https://blog.coursera.org">Coursera Blog</a>.</p>

## Summary
The article discusses changes in U.S. federal borrowing rules for graduate education, emphasizing the need for affordability by design. It suggests that students should focus on flexibility, transparency, and long-term value to assess if a degree is a worthwhile investment.

## Key Points
- Federal borrowing rules for graduate education are changing in the U.S.
- There is a shift towards designing programs for affordability beyond sticker price.
- Key aspects include flexibility, transparency, and long-term value.
- Students should evaluate the investment worth of their degrees.


